  "notes": "The stanza of the Hebrew letter HE — psalm 118 is an alphabetic acrostic of twenty-two stanzas of eight verses, one per letter, and every verse of this one begins with H in the Hebrew. Verse 34 is the verse this edition is named from: DA MIHI INTELLECTUM, ET SCRUTABOR LEGEM TUAM. Three constructions are worth naming. LEGEM (w001) is not the object of PONE: the object is VIAM (w005), and legem stands with pone — legem ponere renders the single Greek verb meaning to lay down as law, so the way is what is being laid down, and legem says what it is laid down as. EXQUIRAM (w009) and CUSTODIAM (w020) have forms shared by the future indicative and present subjunctive. This edition reads both as futures. For EXQUIRAM that reading agrees with the speaker's resolved purpose in the Hebrew text and the unambiguous SERVABO of the Nova Vulgata; EAM (w010) refers to VIAM, as the corresponding Greek pronoun refers to HODON. For CUSTODIAM the coordinate SCRUTABOR, unambiguously future, decides it — the same rule that reads accipiam as future beside invocabo in the priest's Communion verses (Ps 115). SUSPICATUS SUM (w068-w069) is a deponent perfect: passive in form, active in meaning, so it says I have feared and not I have been suspected. In the Roman breviary this stanza's verses open the psalmody of Sunday Terce, whose antiphon is drawn from verse 35; the psalm itself runs across all four of Sunday's little hours; it carries no speaker, because a psalm said in choir has none this edition would record.",
  "source": {
    "method": "Verified by collation against the two independent witnesses below: zero substantive divergence over all eight verses. The only differences are the consonantal i, which witness do prints as j (iustificatiónum, iudícia, iucúnda), and punctuation.",
